                        "entry_id": "37a2c0a8-d7c5-3c51-9c4c-3a9ca44a700f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Membre d’un ordre d’insectes, ptérygotes, néoptères, paranéoptères, ressemblant à des poux ailés, au développement de type hémimétabole."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0e6eeb36-b968-41f6-9a36-82ab911dd1ef",
                                "sense_id": "b31bf7a0-0bb0-3ce9-b764-9dcd06481ca3",
                                "text": "Plus loin, des psocoptères se sont répartis autour des lichens et des mousses pour les brouter."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6789ec62-6a43-4a2a-b857-68e54b7823d4",
                        "source_entry_id": "37a2c0a8-d7c5-3c51-9c4c-3a9ca44a700f",
                        "source_sense_id": "b31bf7a0-0bb0-3ce9-b764-9dcd06481ca3",
                        "target_lang_word": "psocid",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "psocid",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20dc879b-b62a-3d60-92b7-5c43c0518346",
                                        "entry_id": "99c9405f-f1ab-36ab-a4d2-ed318f46f1b0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any insect of the family Psocidae."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2488d35e-4eea-4484-a0ff-3d1b9b025dab",
                                                "sense_id": "20dc879b-b62a-3d60-92b7-5c43c0518346",
                                                "text": "Psocids (New 1987) are free-living insects that feed on microflora and organic debris on surfaces of vegetation or on other surfaces.[…]Psocids are frequently gregarious as nymphs or adults and may even group together under a communal web."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4c2a1d9c-2ac3-4875-97a3-7f0ab4fa37e8",
                                                "sense_id": "20dc879b-b62a-3d60-92b7-5c43c0518346",
                                                "text": "The houe psocid, Liposcelis bostrychophilus, is a tropical species, but it occurs primarily in households in temperate regions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "797477a1-908b-41e4-ac66-4ad93f5b1dab",
                                                "sense_id": "20dc879b-b62a-3d60-92b7-5c43c0518346",
                                                "text": "Nymphs and adults of psocids are regularly found in caves. Psyllipsocidae (in Europe the most common psocid in caves is Psyllipsocus ramburii) and Prionoglarididae are perhaps particularly characteristic of caves.[…]In the case of psocids, it has been suggested that the Psyllipsocidae (an ancient group) may have survived by avoiding competition with more recent forms by inhabitibg caves or similar habitats."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
